          "source_quote": "ὁ δὲ ἐρᾶν με γυναικὸς ἀληθῶς ἀναπεισθεὶς ἔφη",
          "english_quote": "Fully convinced that I truly loved a woman",
          "issue": "The draft supplies both complete conviction through “Fully” and genuine love through “truly.” The Greek supplies one ἀληθῶς, which can qualify either the loving or the conviction; ἀναπεισθείς does not independently establish the added degree of completeness. This attachment uncertainty is not discussed in the notes.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “Convinced that I was truly in love with a woman” without “Fully.” Add a note preserving the alternative “truly convinced that I was in love with a woman,” explaining that the main rendering attaches ἀληθῶς to the supposed love because Appion is interpreting Clement’s affected behavior.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "καὶ ἅπαξ ἐπιτυχών, ἐπαυσάμην τοῦ ἔρωτος.",
          "english_quote": "once I had succeeded, I stopped being in love.",
          "issue": "Here “once” functions as an English temporal conjunction, meaning “after” or “when.” It does not explicitly preserve the numerical force of ἅπαξ: Appion succeeded once and then ceased being in love.",
          "suggested_revision": "Replace this wording with “after I had succeeded once, I stopped being in love,” or another rendering that explicitly preserves a single success. The preceding interpretation of πεισθεὶς and its modifiers is defensible and need not be replaced merely because its disclosed alternative remains possible.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
